<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Legnica</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1241">1241</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Liegnitz, Battle of Wahlstatt · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mongol-invasion-of-poland">First Mongol invasion of Poland</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> was fought on 9 April 1241 near Legnica in Silesia, where a Mongol army defeated the forces of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a> of Silesia, who was killed. It was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-europe">Mongol invasion of Europe</a>. The Mongol detachment then moved south to join the main army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s death weakened Silesia and furthered the fragmentation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnica.jpg" alt="Battle of Legnica" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Legnica1241-From_Legend_of_Saint_Hedwig.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> considered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> their subjects, but the Cumans fled west into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV of Hungary</a> rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a>'s ultimatum to surrender them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> planned the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Batu and Subutai were to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, while a third army under Baidar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orda-khan">Orda Khan</a> and Kadan struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as a diversion. Baidar and Kadan sacked Sandomierz, defeated Polish armies at Tursko and Chmielnik, and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> on 24 March. They failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, and when they heard that Wenceslaus I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> was two days away with an army more than twice the size of Henry's, they turned to intercept Henry before the two could unite.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a> of Silesia commanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Moravians, with Bavarian miners from Goldberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> from Greater Poland, an army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-the-fat">Mieszko II the Fat</a> and a small contingent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a>. Estimates of his army range from 2,000 to as many as 25,000 men, and the Mongol force was at most 25,000 horsemen, perhaps far fewer. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreats</a> to draw the Polish cavalry away from its infantry and then attacked from the flanks, with a smokescreen to hide their movements.</p><p>Henry's army was almost destroyed, and Henry was killed while trying to flee. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> paraded his head on a spear before the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a>. Estimates of European casualties range from 2,000 to 40,000. Mongol losses are unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Wenceslaus I fell back to gather reinforcements on hearing of the defeat. His much larger force routed the Mongol vanguard that overtook it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klodzko">Kłodzko</a>, but since Baidar and Kadan had orders only to divert, they avoided a clash with the Bohemian army and sacked small towns. They then headed south to join Batu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a>, who had defeated the Hungarians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohi">Battle of Mohi</a>. Later raids reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1259–1260 and 1287, the second unsuccessfully. The battle is commemorated on the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> with the inscription LEGNICA 9 IV 1241.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Legnica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jin state of northern China had fallen in 1234; Central Asia and much of Iran had been overrun; and in a single week of April 1241 Mongol armies destroyed a Polish army at <b>Legnica</b> and the army of Hungary at Mohi.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1241 Henry II was killed fighting the Mongols at the <b>Battle of Legnica</b>, a disaster for the duchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Duchy of Silesia</a></li></ul></section>
